<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nalvadi Krishnaraja Wadiyar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1894 to 1940, ruling with full powers from 1902. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-visvesvaraya">M. Visvesvaraya</a> and Mirza Ismail, he made Mysore a model princely state, building dams and a hydroelectric plant, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-mysore">University of Mysore</a> and promoting industry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> called him a 'Rajarshi', a saintly king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iv.jpg" alt="Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV" width="250" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nalvadi_Krishnaraja_Wodeyar_1881-1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Krishnaraja Wadiyar IV was born on 4 June 1884 in Mysore Palace, the son of Maharaja Chamarajendra Wadiyar X and Maharani Kempananjammanni Devi. After his father died suddenly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1894, his mother ruled as regent while he was a boy. As the fourth king of the name he is known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> as Nalwadi Krishnaraja Wadiyar, from nālvaḍi, the fourth.</p><p>He was educated at Lokaranjan Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> under P. Raghavendra Rao, studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> beside Western subjects, horse-riding and Indian and Western classical music. Sir Stuart Fraser of the Bombay Civil Service trained him in administration, and long tours of the state taught him the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a>, the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, invested him with full ruling powers in 1902 at Jaganmohana Palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Mysore Representative Assembly, founded in 1881, was enlarged in his reign and became bicameral in 1907 with the creation of the Mysore Legislative Council. Bangalore had street lights from 5 August 1905, and princes from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were sent to Mysore for administrative training. He abolished child marriage for girls below the age of eight, promoted girls' education, offered scholarships to widows and encouraged spinning, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> praised him.</p><p>He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-mysore">University of Mysore</a>, the first university chartered by an Indian province, co-founded Banaras Hindu University, and was first chancellor of both. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institute-of-science">Indian Institute of Science</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> began its work in 1911 with his gift of 371 acres of land and funds. He played eight instruments, among them the veena, sitar and saxophone, wrote poems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, and hosted musicians of the Agra Gharana and Abdul Karim Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1900 he married Pratapa Kumari Devi, youngest daughter of Rana Bane Singh of Vana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>, the first marriage between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> royal family and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> house of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; they had no children. He died of a heart attack on 3 August 1940, when his fortune was estimated at US$400 million, and was succeeded by his nephew Jayachamaraja Wadiyar. In 1930 John Sankey called Mysore the best administered state in the world, and he is often regarded as the father of modern Mysore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krishna Raja Wadiyar IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV</b> (ruled 1894–1940, with full powers from 1902) and diwans such as M. Visvesvaraya, Mysore built the Shivanasamudra hydroelectric plant, the Krishna Raja Sagara dam, the University of Mysore and industries in Bangalore, which also hosted the Indian Institute of Science.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Bengaluru Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.31,76.65&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Mysore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6656 1705 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7106 1975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7106 1975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.31,76.65&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
